ABOUT: Welcome to the commentary track!

rtn:V-VERSION: Print the title, release, and serial number. This release is branded as “interactive fiction”, replacing the “INTERLOGIC Fantasy” label of the original release.

src:VERBS-301: The source code says “registered trademark”, but the compiled game file prints only “trademark”. The source must have been edited after the game was last compiled.

src:VERBS-810: The source code says “registered trademark”, but the compiled game file prints only “trademark”. The source must have been edited after the game was last compiled.

src:MAIN-52: The [original design document|https://github.com/historicalsource/enchanter/blob/master/enchanter.txt] refers to him only as “the Warlock”. The name “Krill” must have come later.
	I really have no idea if you’re meant to think of malevolent [brine shrimp|https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krill].

src:MAIN-55: “Krill” may be intended to be serious, but “Belboz” is at least a little bit silly.
	The name comes from the same syllable bucket as “foo”, “bar”, “Frobozz”, “Borphee”, and the eight [reset buttons|relative:../suspended/#OBJ:CIRCLE1] in *Suspended*.

obj:BELBOZ: Yes, Belboz is a Necromancer. Apparently Warlocks are disreputable, but Necromancers can be stand-up members of society.
	To be fair, your talent for fatal blunder gives Belboz’s talents a good workout. See [rtn:JIGS-UP].


GRAMMAR-LEGEND: This is the list of verb forms which the game recognizes. (Simple directions like `NORTH` are a special case; they’re not listed here.) These are compiled from the [SYNTAX|~src:SYNTAX-69] file.
	Objects in a verb line have various flags which give hints about their usage. An object attribute (like `LIGHTBIT`) means that if the player doesn’t name an object, the parser will try to guess one with that attribute.
	The other flag letters have specific meanings: **m**ultiple objects are accepted; the player must **h**ave the object; the player will automatically **t**ake the object; the object should be **i**n the room or **o**n the ground; the object should be **p**ossessed or **c**arried in a container. (Yes, they’re confusing. Infocom’s own docs say “No one really understands them except Stu” (Galley).)

attr:RLANDBIT: Indicates rooms; but also used as a hack in grammar lines, where it means the object is optional (but the preposition before it is required). This allows the parser to accept commands like [LOOK AROUND|~src:SYNTAX-295].

glob:P-SYNTAX: The grammar line found when the most recent command was parsed. (Except for simple direction commands, which are a special case.) See the Grammar tab.

rtn:V-CHASTISE: This is the response if you type `LOOK OBJECT` instead of `LOOK AT OBJECT` or `EXAMINE OBJECT`.
	Modern IF tools like Inform 7 have generally gone back to accepting `LOOK OBJECT` as a synonym. Honestly, I’m with Infocom on this one.


OBJTREE-LEGEND: In this list, `Rooms` are rooms and `Items` are regular items. (Sometimes portable, sometimes not.)
	`Scen` indicates scenery objects. These exist in multiple places; for example, the [~obj:HILLS] are visible in many outdoors rooms.
	`Glob` indicates a global object, which is *always* visible, or at least mentionable. For example, [~obj:GLOBAL-SLEEP] is global, so you can always type `GO TO SLEEP`.

obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS: This is a container for scenery objects like [~obj:HILLS], which are visible from many locations in the game.

src:GLOBALS-31: This refers to the word `ZZMGCK` in [~obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS], above.
	Why is this synonym necessary? Not clear! Most Infocom games have it (or a variation), but some say “No, this synonym doesn’t need to exist” instead.
	If you type `EXAMINE ZZMGCK`, the parser finds [~obj:LOCAL-GLOBALS]. But that object is nameless, so you get a garbage reply. Go on, try it.

obj:PLAYER: Yes, the player’s printed name is “cretin”, although the game rarely says so. (The error for boarding a vehicle you’re already in mentions it.) This sort of sarcastic insult hasn’t aged too well, but MIT culture had a lot of it. Compare [*glob:WINNER], and also the old MIT [Jargon File|https://jargon-file.org/archive/jargon-1.0.0.33.dos.txt].
	Note that if you refer to `ME`, you get the [*obj:ME] global object, not the `PLAYER`.
	See also the [obj:ADVENTURER], which – for this one game – is an NPC.

obj:ME: A global object which is always in scope. Commands referring to `ME` hit this, rather than [*obj:PLAYER], which is a stub that moves around the map.
	Because of this, parser messages mostly refer to you as “you”, even though the [*obj:PLAYER]’s printed name is “cretin”.

glob:WINNER: `WINNER` is the actor in the current command.
	`WINNER` is usually [~obj:PLAYER], but for a command like `TURTLE, GO EAST` it shifts to the named NPC.
	“Winner” is just as sarcastic as “[cretin|*obj:PLAYER]”, but from the other side.

glob:HERE: The location of the [glob:WINNER].


rtn:GO: Set up the world at the start of the game. Start the timers. Print the banner. Set all those useful globals like [~glob:HERE] and [~glob:WINNER].

rtn:JIGS-UP: This routine handles the [~glob:WINNER]’s demise, whether that is the [~obj:PLAYER] or an NPC obeying a command.
	If the [~glob:WINNER] is protected by `OZMOO` (see [glob:DEATH-CHEATED]), the death is avoided – unless the second argument is explicitly false (`<>`), which indicates a death too terrible for `OZMOO`.
	Failing all that, Belboz will resurrect you three times (see [glob:DEATHS]). Or really four, but the last time you are demoted and the game ends.

rtn:FINISH: This is called when the game is really, finally over. Display your score and offer the traditional `RESTORE`/`RESTART`/`QUIT` option.

glob:DEATH-CHEATED: The person currently protected by `OZMOO`.

glob:DEATHS: The number of times Belboz has interceded to prevent your death.


glob:LIT: Whether the current location has light. Set by calling the [rtn:LIT?] routine.

rtn:LIT?: Determine whether a given location has light. This is true if [glob:ALWAYS-LIT] is set, or if the room or anything in it has [attr:ONBIT].
	We also check for rooms which have [attr:LIGHTBIT] but *not* [attr:ONBIT]. Such rooms are only lit during the day. However, at some point the developers seem to have decided that nights always have [starlight|~src:VERBS-90] and [moonlight|~src:VERBS-93]. So all outdoor rooms have [attr:ONBIT].
	This routine is used to set the [glob:LIT] variable.

glob:ALWAYS-LIT: If set, the world is always illuminated.
	This is set if you `FROTZ ME` (see [here|~src:GLOBALS-140]). But there’s no way to turn it off. (Unlike other `FROTZ`ed objects; see [attr:LIGHTBIT].)

attr:ONBIT: Set for glowing objects and permanently-illuminated rooms. Compare [attr:LIGHTBIT].

attr:LIGHTBIT: This indicates objects which can be switched on and off. When on, they will also have [attr:ONBIT].
	(`FROTZ`ed objects have `LIGHTBIT` temporarily, which means they can be un-`FROTZ`ed with a simple `TURN OFF` command. This is not intuitive, but it seems to be deliberate. Except that if you `FROTZ` *yourself*, this doesn’t work, which is even more unintuitive.)
	Outdoor rooms, and rooms which can see the sky, also have `LIGHTBIT`. This seems to have been intended to support rooms that are dark only at night. However, this feature is not used; see [rtn:LIT?]

attr:BURNBIT: Set for objects which the player can `BURN`. (It might be a bad idea, but you can do it.)
	This is different from [attr:LIGHTBIT], objects which the player can `TURN ON`.

attr:FLAMEBIT: Most Infocom games use this to indicate objects which are on fire. However, *Enchanter* does not use `FLAMEBIT`. The [~obj:ETERNAL-FLAME], the only flaming object in the game, is handled as a special case by [~rtn:V-BURN].

glob:LOAD-ALLOWED: The maximum weight you can carry. This decreases as you become tired; see [rtn:I-TIRED]. When you rest, it is reset to [glob:LOAD-MAX].

glob:LOAD-MAX: The maximum weight you can carry at full strength. (Compare [glob:LOAD-ALLOWED].)

glob:P-WON: Set if the parser successfully parsed a command. (As opposed to a blank line or an error.)
	No relation to whether the *player* has won.


TIMERS-LEGEND: A list of the timers running (or paused) in the timer table ([~glob:C-TABLE]). Positive numbers count down (while enabled) and call the named function after that many turns. Negative numbers are called every turn while the timer is enabled.
	This list only grows as you play; timers can be added, started, and stopped, but not removed.

glob:C-INTS: Indicates how much of the timer table ([*glob:C-TABLE]) is used. This counts down from `C-TABLELEN`, which is 180.

glob:C-DEMONS: Indicates how much of the timer table is used for daemons.
	The difference between daemons and timers is that timers run every turn; daemons run every *input*, even if no turn passes. But *Enchanter* does not use daemons.

glob:C-TABLE: The timer table. See the “Timers” tab for a current listing.

rtn:I-TIME: The time-of-day timer. This advances [glob:TOD] every turn.
	When [glob:TOD] reaches 128 (roughly 24 in-game hours), it’s reset to zero and a new day begins. Krill’s power increases: [glob:LOSSAGE] goes up by one, and [glob:DUSK] and [glob:NIGHTFALL] are brought forward by 20 (about two game hours). The [glob:MUNCHED] and [glob:MOLESTED] probabilities also increase.
	Time also advances when you `SLEEP`. This is handled elsewhere; see [rtn:V-SLEEP]. But there is a [serious bug|src:SLEEP-194] in the sleep-time logic.

rtn:V-SLEEP: This handles the effects of sleeping. (Both the `SLEEP` verb and [collapsing from exhaustion|rtn:I-TIRED].) The optional `TOLD?` argument just indicates whether the game has already told you “the bed is very comfortable”.
	Sleeping advances time by [~const:MOVES-PER-SLEEP] turns, and many beneficial effects. [glob:REAL-SPELL-MAX] increases by one; the weariness penalties to [glob:LOAD-ALLOWED], [glob:FUMBLE-NUMBER], and [glob:FUMBLE-PROB] are reset. Also, [~glob:DREAMS] are important. On the down side, you forget all your spells, and there’s a chance that monsters will [rob|glob:MOLESTED] or [kill|glob:MUNCHED] you.
	There is, however, a [serious bug|src:SLEEP-194] in the `SLEEP` logic.

src:SLEEP-194: The time-of-day ([glob:TOD]) is increased by [const:MOVES-PER-SLEEP] turns. The `MOD` ensures that values beyond 128 wrap around to zero, as they should.
	But there is a serious gap in this logic! If `TOD` wraps around here, the game does *not* apply the normal end-of-day logic from [rtn:I-TIME]. This means that Krill’s power does not increase. [glob:NIGHTFALL] does not shift, [glob:LOSSAGE] does not advance, and so on.
	This means that if you are careful to only sleep at “midnight” or later, you can block Krill’s dominion and play for as many days as you like. Until you run out of bread, at least.
	(Rooms are still ruined by [rtn:I-MUNG-ROOM], though. That’s handled as a special case in `V-SLEEP`.)

glob:DREAMS: A list of prosaic dreams. Significant dreams are handled in [rtn:V-SLEEP].

src:SLEEP-326: This may be an underhanded reference to *Infidel*, Infocom’s next-coming game.

glob:TOD: The time of day. This runs on a scale of dawn (0) to the following dawn (128), at which time it’s reset to zero. (See [rtn:I-TIME].) The game does not deal in clock time, but [glob:TIME-TABLE] gives an idea of how the day runs. Five turns approximate an hour of game time.
	[glob:DUSK] and [glob:NIGHTFALL] come earlier every day as Krill’s power increases, but the total length of the day does not change.

glob:TIME-TABLE: A table of time descriptions. These follow the [glob:TOD] scale, where dawn is 0 and the following dawn in 128. Each line is 10 [glob:TOD] units, about two hours.
	The `TIME` command reports these strings. They do *not* take account of the advancing [glob:DUSK] and [glob:NIGHTFALL] as Krill’s power increases.

glob:DUSK: The expected time of sunset, as compared to [glob:TOD]. This starts unrealistically late — about 10 PM if you take the [glob:TIME-TABLE] comments seriously. It decreases by 20 every day (see [rtn:I-TIME]).

glob:NIGHTFALL: The expected time of full night, as compared to [glob:TOD]. This starts unrealistically late — about 1 AM if you take the [glob:TIME-TABLE] comments seriously. This makes for better drama, of course. Since `NIGHTFALL` decreases by 20 every day (see [rtn:I-TIME]), you have five dwindling game days before doom arrives.

glob:LOSSAGE: How much notice Krill has taken of your presence. It increases by 1 every day (see [rtn:I-TIME]). It also increases when you disturb the [~obj:TERROR], and if you foolishly try to `GNUSTO` an uncopiable scroll.
	The [~obj:GANG-OF-FOUR] will follow you longer as `LOSSAGE` increases. See [rtn:I-GANG].

glob:MOLESTED: The chance of lurking creatures robbing you in your sleep. If this happens, there is also a chance (based on [glob:MUNCHED]) that they will eat you.
	This increases every day.

glob:MUNCHED: The chance of lurking creatures devouring you in your sleep, once they run across you. (See [glob:MOLESTED]. If they don’t eat you, they will just steal your stuff.)
	This increases every day.

rtn:I-MUNG-ROOM: A timer which periodically applies ruination ([attr:RMUNGBIT]) to the rooms in [~glob:MUNG-ROOM-TABLE], one by one. When you [SLEEP|rtn:V-SLEEP], three go in a row.

rtn:I-SCURRY: Timer for occasional atmospheric messages.

rtn:I-TIRED: The sleepiness timer. As you get more tired, your weight limit ([~glob:LOAD-ALLOWED]) and spell limit ([~glob:SPELL-MAX]) decrease; your chance of fumbling objects ([~glob:FUMBLE-NUMBER], [~glob:FUMBLE-PROB]) increases. When [glob:AWAKE] reaches 10, you collapse.

glob:AWAKE: How tired you are. -1 means fully awake. After [const:MOVES-PER-DAY] turns, the [rtn:I-TIRED] timer increases `AWAKE` periodically. When it reaches 10, you collapse.

const:MOVES-PER-DAY: How long you can be awake before you start feeling sleepy. See [rtn:I-TIRED].

const:MOVES-PER-SLEEP: How long you sleep for when you go to sleep. See [~rtn:V-SLEEP].

rtn:I-GUARDS-ARRIVE: Timer for the [~obj:GANG-OF-FOUR] coming your way. This has a 30% chance of starting each turn you are in the [~obj:LIBRARY] or [~obj:BANQUET-HALL]. They will arrive on the next turn ([rtn:I-GANG]).

rtn:I-GANG: Timer for the [~obj:GANG-OF-FOUR] trying to capture you. If you stand still, they will do so (unless you have cast `MELBOR`). Otherwise, they will follow you through any number of ruined rooms (see [rtn:I-MUNG-ROOM]) and up to [glob:LOSSAGE] unspoiled rooms as well.


attr:RMUNGBIT: Indicates ruined objects and rooms.

prop:MUNGDESC: A few rooms have a different description when ruined. This is it.

attr:VILLAIN: Generally set for living beings, although they may or may not be villainous. The portraits in the [~obj:GALLERY] have it as well.
	Compare [~attr:VICBIT].
	The [~obj:PLAYER] has the `VILLAIN` flag, which leads to a few buggy messages. Try `HELLO ME`, for example.

attr:VICBIT: Generally set for living beings, though not the [~obj:PLAYER]. The [~obj:MAGIC-ROPE] and the [~obj:MIRROR] have it as well.
	This is almost the same as [~attr:VILLAIN], but some verbs use [~attr:VILLAIN] and some `VICBIT`, with no obvious pattern.

attr:VICTIM: This flag is only set for the [~obj:DRAGON], and it’s never used. It’s almost certainly a typo for [attr:VICBIT].

src:STAIR-391: The [attr:VICTIM] flag is only set for the [~obj:DRAGON], and it’s never used. It’s almost certainly a typo for [attr:VICBIT].

attr:SPELLBIT: Set for spell objects, naturally. (Compare [attr:SCROLLBIT].)

attr:SCROLLBIT: Set for scrolls, naturally. (Compare [attr:SPELLBIT].)

attr:POINTBIT: Set for the nine point objects on the [~obj:TMAP].

prop:COUNT: For spell objects, tells how many times you have the spell memorized. For rooms, distinguishes the four mirror rooms.

attr:TREASURE: Set for obviously valuable objects. You don’t care, but the [obj:ADVENTURER] does.

glob:FUMBLE-NUMBER: If you have more than this many objects, trying to take another has a [chance|src:VERBS-643] of failing.
	This starts at seven but decreases as you tire. See [rtn:I-TIRED].

glob:FUMBLE-PROB: The chance of failing to take another object, if you have more than [~glob:FUMBLE-NUMBER].
	This starts at eight (per object) but increases as you tire. See [rtn:I-TIRED].

src:VERBS-643: The “holding too many things” message is probabilistic. The more objects you have past [~glob:FUMBLE-NUMBER], the greater the chance ([~glob:FUMBLE-PROB]) of failing to pick up another.


glob:REAL-SPELL-MAX: The maximum number of spells you can memorize at once (ignoring `GNUSTO`) when fully rested. This starts at four, but increases by one every time you [sleep|rtn:V-SLEEP]. (Compare [glob:SPELL-MAX].)

glob:SPELL-MAX: The number of spells you can currently keep memorized (ignoring `GNUSTO`). When fully rested, this is the same as [glob:REAL-SPELL-MAX], but it decreases as you tire. (See [rtn:I-TIRED].)

glob:SPELL-ROOM: The number of additional spells you can memorize, beyond the ones you already know.
	This value is maintained in parallel with [glob:SPELL-MAX] and your spell list. Handling it this way is redundant. It sets my teeth on edge but I’m not aware of any bugs in the logic.


obj:ADVENTURER: The Adventurer is of course a farago of in-jokes about the *Zork* games. (My favorite is [glob:SAILOR?])

glob:SAILOR?: Set if the Adventurer has said “[Hello, sailor|~src:PURLOINED-753]” to you.
	If you give him an object on the very next turn, he [smiles|~src:PURLOINED-686], because he thinks he’s figured out what `HELLO SAILOR` is for! This is a wonderful callback to [Zork 3|relative:../zork3/#RTN:SAILOR-F].

src:PURLOINED-686: See [glob:SAILOR?]

obj:PURLOINED-ROOM: “Purloined” in the sense of the Purloined Letter. The solution is right in front of you, but you can’t see it because of all the magical frippery.

rtn:NO-ILLUSIONS: The point here is that the Adventurer is too mundane for the illusion magic to affect.

### purple shift?

src:PURLOINED-787: See the response to typing `MUMBLE`, either [here|~RTN:V-MUMBLE] or in [Zork 1|relative:../zork1/#RTN:V-MUMBLE].


### terror logic


rtn:I-TAKE-TO-ALTAR: This scene was prefigured at the [Scenic View|relative:../zork3/#RTN:ZORK-IV-F] in *Zork 3*. But in *Enchanter*, you can survive the experience.

glob:LETTER-OF-TRANSIT: If you survive being sacrificed ([rtn:I-TAKE-TO-ALTAR]), the game gives you a few turns to leave the Temple. While `LETTER-OF-TRANSIT` is set, the evil creatures will not molest you.


const:ZORK-NUMBER: The number of the Zorks shall be three! (Four, sir.) Four!
	Infocom had a running argument, or running gag, about whether *Enchanter* was really “Zork IV” or not. But the [Scenic View|relative:../zork3/#OBJ:VIEW-ROOM] in *Zork 3* referred to it as such.

src:VERBS-1157: This last message is not compiled into the game file. It’s effectively an `ELSE` for the “`PRSO` in `WINNER`” `COND`, which already has an `ELSE` (`T`) clause directly above (the “catches fire and is consumed” response). So it can never run, and the compiler optimizes it out.

rtn:V-GROSS-OUT: The command `GROSS OUT` is not implemented in any other Infocom game. Why here? Perhaps because there is a [~obj:SILVER-SPOON], and some tester tried `GAG ME WITH A SPOON` (which is also supported).





